      "reference": "2.22",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": false,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "Ἄκουσον, φίλτατε ἡμῖν ἀδελφέ",
          "english_quote": "Listen, dearest brother",
          "issue": "The explicit plural relationship expressed by ἡμῖν, “to us,” is omitted. Aquila addresses the brother on behalf of the group, not merely with an unspecified affectionate superlative.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “Listen, brother dearest to us,” or another natural formulation that explicitly preserves “to us.”",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ὡς πιστῶς πιστεύοντες ἀπόλλυνται",
          "english_quote": "trustingly believe the hope and judgment which he says will somehow come to pass, and they perish.",
          "issue": "The draft turns the participial qualification into an independent assertion coordinated with “they perish,” leaving ὡς unrepresented. The notes discuss object attachment but not whether ὡς characterizes their believing stance or presents it as supposed. Also, “trustingly believe” does not visibly retain the cognate wording of πιστῶς πιστεύοντες; the existing rationale needs comparison with “believing faithfully.”",
          "suggested_revision": "Under the draft’s disclosed object attachment, compare “… perish as people believing faithfully in the hope and judgment which he says will somehow come to pass.” Explain the function of ὡς and preserve the viable subjective alternative, “as though believing faithfully,” without presenting that alternative as the only interpretation. Submit the chosen revision for fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "2.23",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἧς ἡ πορεία τοῦ μηνὸς οὐ τέλειον ποιεῖται τὸν δρόμον",
          "english_quote": "since the moon’s journey does not complete its course during the month.",
          "issue": "The draft defensibly treats τοῦ μηνὸς as temporal, “during the month,” but does not disclose the material alternative attaching it to τὸν δρόμον: “the month’s course.” The latter concerns the lunar movement not making up a full monthly course, rather than an explicitly lunar course left unfinished during a month. This distinction matters to the preceding thirty-member and half-unit comparison. It is an interpretive attachment question, not evidence of source corruption.",
          "suggested_revision": "Add a note preserving the alternative “whose movement does not cover the month’s course in full” and explain why the main text chooses the temporal attachment. Compare that source-transparent alternative with the existing wording before deciding whether to retain or revise the main text; any replacement requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "2.24",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "τοῦ μὴ αὐτὸν εἶναι νομίζειν τὸν ἑστῶτα",
          "english_quote": "so that he was thought not to be the Standing One.",
          "issue": "The main rendering is defensible with μή understood as negating the embedded identification, but the existing note discusses attachment without disclosing the material scope alternative: not thinking him to be the Standing One, rather than positively thinking him not to be it. These are different representations of the group’s judgment.",
          "suggested_revision": "Preserve the alternative “so that people would no longer think him to be the Standing One” in a note and explain the choice to take μή with the embedded identification rather than with νομίζειν. This ordinary scope ambiguity does not require print verification or automatically block publication.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "εἰς τὸν ἑαυτοῦ τῆς ὑπονοίας τόπον ἔστησε τὸν Σίμωνα",
          "english_quote": "installed Simon in the place of esteem that had been his own.",
          "issue": "“Installed” conveys appointment to the position, but conceals the standing language of ἔστησε within the sustained Standing One, lowering, standing, and falling pattern. The note acknowledges that pattern without comparing a source-transparent main-text candidate, as the supplied wording policy requires.",
          "suggested_revision": "Compare “made Simon stand in the place in their estimation that he himself had held.” Retain the disclosed alternatives for τῆς ὑπονοίας and explain any decision to keep “installed” despite the standing pattern. The proposed wording is not yet accepted and must receive fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
